---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import QuickAnswer from '../components/QuickAnswer.astro';
import FaqBlock from '../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

const path = '/new-tenant-lock-change-london';
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Should I change the locks after moving in?',
    a: 'It is worth doing. You rarely know how many keys to the old lock exist — previous occupants, their friends, cleaners, contractors or agents may have copies. Changing the euro cylinder means only the keys you are handed work.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I rent — do I need my landlord’s permission?',
    a: 'Check your tenancy agreement. Shelter says tenants can change locks if they feel unsafe provided the old locks are kept to refit at the end of the tenancy; landlord bodies say many agreements require permission. Asking in writing and offering your landlord a key avoids most disputes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does it take?',
    a: 'The fitting itself is typically 30–45 minutes. Planned bookings are usually within 2–5 working days of your email.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I just bought the property — anything else to check?',
    a: 'Check every door with a euro cylinder, including back, side and garage doors, and look at the cylinder face for three stars. If you also have a mortice lock on a timber door, that is a separate lock we do not change.',
  },
];
---
<ContentLayout
  path={path}
  lead="New flat or house? Until the cylinder is changed, every old key still works. We fit an Avocet ABS 3-star with three new keys — £149 flat."
  audience="Tenants and new homeowners"
  ctaHeading="Moved in? Make the old keys useless."
  ctaBody={`${SITE.price} flat — Avocet ABS cylinder, fitting and three keys. Email your postcode and door type; we reply within 2 hours.`}
>
  <QuickAnswer>
    <p>
      After moving into a London flat or house, changing the euro cylinder is the quickest way to make every
      previous key useless. kianlocks fits an Avocet ABS (TS007 3-star, Sold Secure Diamond) with three new keys for
      <strong>{SITE.price} flat</strong>. Tenants should check their tenancy agreement first and keep the old cylinder to refit when they leave.
    </p>
  </QuickAnswer>

  <h2>Who still has a key to your door?</h2>
  <p>
    Previous tenants or owners, their family and friends, cleaners, dog walkers, builders, letting agents and
    the occasional neighbour “just in case”. You can’t collect them all back — but you can make them all stop working.
  </p>

  <h2>If you rent</h2>
  <p>
    Whether you can change the lock depends on your tenancy agreement. Ask your landlord in writing, keep the
    original cylinder so it can go back at the end of the tenancy, and offer a key. Full detail in
    <a href="/guides/can-tenants-change-locks/">can tenants change the locks?</a>
  </p>

  <h2>If you’ve just bought</h2>
  <p>
    Change every door locked by a euro cylinder — front, back, side and garage. While we’re there we’ll tell you
    what rating each cylinder currently has, so you know where you stand. Each door is {SITE.price}.
  </p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Avocet ABS cylinder, sized to your door.</li>
    <li>Three new keys.</li>
    <li>An itemised invoice naming the product and ratings.</li>
    <li>No callout fee, no hourly labour.</li>
  </ul>

  <FaqBlock faqs={faqs} heading="Moving-in questions" bare />

  <RelatedLinks links={[
    { href: '/guides/can-tenants-change-locks/', title: 'Can tenants change locks?', blurb: 'What the sources say.' },
    { href: '/guides/ts007-3-star-explained/', title: 'Check your lock rating', blurb: 'A 30-second check.' },
    { href: '/areas/', title: 'Areas we cover', blurb: 'Nine North & Central London boroughs.' },
  ]} />
</ContentLayout>
